  <page>
    <exercise>
        <title>A Special Collision</title>
        <p>
            Two objects of equal mass are approaching one another with equal speeds v and collide head-on. 
            By the symmetry of the situation, after the collision their velocities reverse. Using only this 
            information, determine what happens to the objects after a different collision: one of them is at rest, 
            and the other one, initially with speed <m>v</m>, collides head-on with it.
        </p>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Crossing the river</title>
        <p>
            For the swimmer problem we did in class, consider the alternative scenario: what if instead of trying to
            cross the bank in the shortest time, we are trying to cross the bank by swimming the shortest distance? 
            When the swim speed <m>V</m> is larger than the current speed <m>u</m>, we solved this in class. 
            What should you do when <m>v\lt u</m>? There's an elegant solution, but if you go about it by trying to
            directly find the minimum of the distance, try to extremize the simplest quantity.
        </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Catching the bus</title>
        <p>
            You woke up late and are worried about missing the bus. 
            As you approach the bus stop, you spot the bus at a distance <m>a</m> directly west of the bus stop. 
            You are at a distance <m>b</m> directly south of the bus stop, but you're walking through a park and 
            can run in any direction. If the bus drives at speed <m>V</m>, what is the minimum speed <m>u</m> at 
            which you need to run to catch the bus? 
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Using Vectors to Describe Motion</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
             A wheel of radius <m>R</m> is spinning clockwise on a fixed axis with a constant angular velocity 
             <m>\omega</m>. Coordinate axes are chosen as shown, and the axis of the spinning wheel travels
             with uniform velocity <m>\vec{V}_0</m> along the x axis. The value of <m>V_0</m> is an independent 
             parameter, and is not related to <m>\omega</m> in any way. For example, the axis of the wheel is 
             attached to a train car, traveling with that velocity, and the wheel just happens to be spinning
             at some rate.
             </p> 
             <p>
                Imagine painting a particular point on the wheel in red. At t=0, the axis of the wheel is at 
                <m>(0,R)</m> and point you painted is at <m>(0,0)</m> (<q>the ground</q>) 
             </p>

             <image source="images/hwimg/hw2/wheel.svg" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>Add descriptions</shortdescription>
             </image>

        </introduction>
        <task>
            <title>Warm-up: motion of a point on the wheel</title>
            
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the position, velocity and acceleration vectors for the point, as a function of time.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <title>Rolling without slipping</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    If our point on the wheel were to be moving horizontally whenever it touched the ground,
                    there would be a lot of friction between the wheels and the track, which would lead to wear
                    on the wheels. A real wheel often satisfies the <term>no-slip condition</term>, meaning that
                    the point of the wheel touching the ground is never moving horizontally.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Show that this condition is satisfied if <m>V_0=\omega R</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Qualitatively explain why there is no friction between the wheel and the
                        ground only when this condition is satisfied.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write expressions for the position, velocity, and acceleration vectors of the point
                        on the wheel in terms of <m>\omega, R, t</m> only.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the acceleration of the point on the moment it touches the ground?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </task>

        <task>
            <title>A Common Confusion</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The relationships (1) <m>v = \omega R</m> of the speed of a particle going around a circle, and
                    (2) <m>V_0 = \omega R</m> of the previous part look very similar but describe two completely 
                    different things. Don't confuse them!
                </p> 
                <p>
                    To illustrate the difference, consider the following scenario. A motorcyclist revs up the 
                    engine, kicks into gear, the wheel spins up almost instantaneously, and the motorcycle races 
                    off in a puff of smoke, leaving the smell of burnt rubber and a big black mark on the asphalt.
                    Let <m>\omega (t)</m> denote the angular speed of the tire. 
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Which speed in this problem (of what, relative to what?) is related to 
                        <m>\omega</m> by relationship (1), and which by relationship (2)?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Note that of these two relationships, one holds throughout the scene described, and 
                        the other only for a portion of the time! Comment which is which.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
